course: dictionary
lesson: 813
title: Beheading: a real punishment, not a joke
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Beheading is a noun meaning cutting off someone's head as a punishment. It is also a verb, behead, which means cutting off the head of a person or animal. Its form here is the -ing form, so it is the act itself.
explain: Read the sentence «So the beheadings are only shouting.» The word only is your clue: nothing is cut off here, just shouted. So beheadings means the threats of beheading, not the real act. Swap in your meaning: "So the threats are only shouting" still makes sense, so you have the right meaning.
explain: Words can be used as a joke in a story, but this word is real. Beheading was a terrible punishment in history. When you see it in a funny story, remember what it means in real life.
fact: Beheading means cutting off someone's head as a punishment.
fact: Behead is a verb: to behead someone is to cut off their head.
fact: «Anne Boleyn» and «Charles I» were beheaded in history.
fact: In Tudor England an axe was used; in the French Revolution a guillotine was used.
fact: In «Alice in Wonderland», the Queen of Hearts shouts «Off with his head!», but nobody is actually beheaded.
exercise: In history, what is a beheading? | *Cutting off someone's head as a punishment | A loud shout at a party | Cutting someone's hair short | Being sent to bed early as a punishment
exercise: «So the beheadings are only shouting.» What does only tell us? | *That no one is actually beheaded, just threatened | That the axe is sharp | That the beheadings happen every day | That the beheadings are very loud
exercise: Which word in «Anne Boleyn and Charles I were beheaded» is a form of the verb behead? | Charles | Anne | history | *beheaded
test: In «Alice in Wonderland», what does the Queen of Hearts shout? | *«Off with his head!» | «Good morning, everyone!» | «Tea is ready!» | «Let's go home!»
test: In the French Revolution, which tool was used for beheading? | The bow and arrow | *The guillotine | The rope | The frying pan
exam: Why is beheading not a joke in real life? | It means shouting loudly | *It was a real, terrible punishment from history | It is a kind of pet | It is always fun to read about
